<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tell Siran bottle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 600 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tell Siran inscription</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tell Siran bottle is a small bronze bottle found in 1972 at Tell Siran, on the campus of the University of Jordan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a>. Its Ammonite inscription of about 600 BCE celebrates the works of Amminadab, king of the Ammonites, son of Hissalel, listing a vineyard, a garden and pools. It names three generations of Ammonite kings and is one of the longest complete Ammonite texts.</p></div>
